##############################################################################
#
#  Incident template. Copy this file, rename it, fill it in, delete the
#  sections that do not apply. One file per incident.
#
#  Fill order matters. Do the trajectory before the classification, or you
#  will label the incident with the mode you expected and then write a
#  trajectory that agrees with you.
#
#    1. actors and intent      who, and what did they say they were doing
#    2. trajectory             what happened, in order, as a sequence
#    3. the first departure    the earliest step the declared intent cannot justify
#    4. classification         which modes, linked UP into drift-model.n4l
#    5. signals                what a detector could have seen, and when
#    6. what would have caught it
#    7. provenance             source, date, evidence class
#
#  Rules that keep the graph useful:
#
#  * Do not invent new mode: or indicator: nodes here. Link to the ones in
#    drift-model.n4l. If none fit, say so with a question node and raise it.
#  * Signals must be things a machine could observe from outside the agent.
#    "The agent decided to" is not a signal. "A write landed at path X" is.
#  * Record what you do not know. An empty field is a silent claim; a
#    question node is an honest one.
#
##############################################################################

-Incident: SHORT NAME HERE

 #############################################################
 # 1. Actors and declared intent
 #############################################################

 :: incident, actors, TOPIC KEYWORDS FOR SEARCH ::

 incident: SHORT NAME (incdate) YYYY-MM-DD
        "             (report-src) "https://example.com/source"
        "             (evidence) evidence: public reporting
        "             (severity) high
        "             (blast) what was actually damaged, in one line

 incident: SHORT NAME (has-actor) actor: the agent
        "             (has-actor) actor: the principal who tasked it
        "             (has-actor) actor: the party who bore the damage

 actor: the agent (autonomy) autonomy: supervised per action
      "            (principal) actor: the principal who tasked it
      "            (boundary) boundary: what the agent was allowed to reach

 # The single most important line in the file. If the source does not say
 # what the agent was told to do, record that it does not, because an
 # incident with no recoverable declared intent cannot train a detector.

 actor: the agent (declares) intent: WHAT THE AGENT WAS ASKED TO DO
      "            (implies) intent: the unstated constraint everyone assumed
      "            (observed) what it actually did, in one line
      "            (reported-as) what it said it had done, if this differed

 intent: WHAT THE AGENT WAS ASKED TO DO (invariant) invariant: the thing that was supposed to stay true
        "                               (question) was this ever stated to the agent, or only assumed

 #############################################################
 # 2. Trajectory
 #
 #    Sequence mode chains these with then arrows automatically. Keep one
 #    observable step per line. Resist summarising several steps into one,
 #    because the departure usually hides in the summary.
 #############################################################

 :: incident, trajectory, _sequence_ ::

@step1 first observable action
@step2 second observable action
@step3 the step that needed a different goal to justify it
@step4 what that made possible
@step5 the irreversible action
@step6 how it was described afterwards

 -:: _sequence_ ::

 #############################################################
 # 3. The first departure
 #############################################################

 :: incident, departure, analysis ::

 incident: SHORT NAME (departure) $step3.1
 $step3.1 (note) why the declared intent does not justify this step
     "    (drifted-from) intent: WHAT THE AGENT WAS ASKED TO DO
     "    (drifts-to) the goal it was actually serving from here on
     "    (latency) how long until anyone noticed

 #############################################################
 # 4. Classification. Links UP into the reference model.
 #############################################################

 :: incident, classification ::

 incident: SHORT NAME (drift-mode) mode: PICK FROM THE TAXONOMY
        "             (drift-mode) mode: SECONDARY MODE IF ANY
        "             (violates) invariant: the thing that was supposed to stay true

 # Position on the canonical arc, so a live run can be compared to it.

 incident: SHORT NAME (like-drift) arc: the first departure, where a step needs a different goal to justify it

 #############################################################
 # 5. Signals. What a detector could have seen.
 #############################################################

 :: incident, signals, detection ::

 $step3.1 (signal) signal: PICK OR ADD A MACHINE OBSERVABLE SIGNAL
     "    (indicator) indicator: PICK FROM THE REFERENCE MODEL

 incident: SHORT NAME (evaded) guardrail: the control that existed and did not fire
        "             (note) why it did not fire, which is usually more useful than the incident itself

 #############################################################
 # 6. What would have caught it
 #############################################################

 :: incident, prevention, guardrails ::

 incident: SHORT NAME (prevented-by) guardrail: PICK FROM THE REFERENCE MODEL
        "             (checkpoint) checkpoint: the question that would have exposed this at step 3
        "             (response) response: what should happen when this pattern is seen live

 #############################################################
 # 7. Open questions
 #############################################################

 :: incident, open questions ::

 incident: SHORT NAME (question) what is not established by the source
        "             (tbd) what to check before relying on this encoding
